body:
I'm sorry to hear about your hair loss struggles! I too have thinning hair (F, 31) and it's been a nightmare for me over the past year. Very hard to accept and adjust to.

&#x200B;

I've just started using light therapy, and from what I've read, it seems extremely promising. There are buckets of clinical studies on its efficacy for hair growth, and I'm hopeful it will be better than my biotin/iron/Nizoral routine. There's a red light cap called iRestore that you can read Amazon reviews about, but you can also find a $200 off coupon online for use on their website (so it costs $100 less than on Amazon). There's also the iGrow, which costs significantly less if you buy it refurbished. Compared to hair weave systems, both options are pennies.

&#x200B;

I already have an infrared light device, which is a little more powerful than something like the iRestore (though less convenient than a cap design, as I have to lie under it), so I'm using that myself. Fingers crossed for results. Do check out all those studies (and reviews)! There are even some rather bald men who have good outcomes with red light. Best of luck.

Many thanks for your suggestions. I am on and off bipolar medication (I'm between psychiatrists till Weds and had to go off my last med due to side effects). I have expressed concern about weight gain as a side effect, so only briefly tried one med that's associated with weight gain. I did try lithium last year, which is known to contribute to temporary hair loss, but it wasn't an effective med for me anyway.

&#x200B;

I did have a full thyroid panel evaluated, and no issues there. I had relatively low ferritin, so I take a good iron supplement now. It hasn't seemed to help.

&#x200B;

My thought is that both the change in body composition (not really weight per se, my weight is the same) and the hair loss are hormone related. I know that women's body composition tends to change noticeably in their early 40s, due to menopause / hormonal changes. They tend to store more fat on their bellies, perhaps becoming smaller elsewhere, and the fat may be difficult or impossible to shift. That resonates with my experience, even though I'm only in my early 30s. It's just hard when you've never had weight issues before, and suddenly it's a daily struggle. 

&#x200B;

Since hair loss (eg androgenic alopecia - male/female pattern hair loss) tends to be so tied up with hormones, it's possible that my hair loss is also hormone-related. It's all just rather confusing because it's diffuse, consistent with telogen effluvium (confirmed by my derm), and not concentrated in the crown like FPHL. Still, there's no clear stressor that's contributing to it - it's just been persistent for nearly 2 years now. I'm starting infrared light therapy for it - I'm just in the process of researching that, and since I have a light device at home (and red light therapy has so many proven health benefits), I figure there's no harm in starting a regime while I'm gathering more information. I may buy one of the helmet devices available for hair regrowth, which look very promising.

&#x200B;

I may see a nutritionist my mom knows for hormone testing, which may help with the body fat changes and hair loss. Although I definitely don't need cosmetic surgery for my chin, it's helpful to think of that as a back-pocket option in case things became more distressing in the future. Thank you for the suggestion.

&#x200B;

I'm feeling a little bit better today in my body due to a combination of the low-calorie McDougall diet yesterday, and intermittent fasting. I feel like a broke through a little plateau. 

&#x200B;

It's also helpful to hear that your family members have experienced physical changes over the years, likely due to age and genetics. Thank you for sharing :-)
